in this video we're going to talk about  how to complete the square so if you're  given a quadratic like x squared plus 6x  minus seven equals zero and you want to  solve this equation by completing the  square the first step is to get the  variables on one side of the equation  and the numbers on the opposite side so  I'm going to do is I'm going to add 7 to  both sides of this equation and if we do  that we're left with x squared well 6x  equals 7 now what you want to do is you  want to take the coefficient the number  in front of the X to the first term and  you want to divide that by 2 and square  it so we take 6 divided by 2 and we  square that quantity so that's going to  be 3 squared which equals 9 so we want  to do is we want to add 9 to the left  side of the equation as well as to the  right side of the equation to keep it  balanced now what we can do is we can  factor this left side of the equation  and it's going to be a perfect square  and when you factor this is going to be  ax and it'll always be 1/2 of this  middle coefficient so half of 6 is going  to be positive 3 if this was minus 6x  then it would be negative 3 and that  equals 16 so now we've completed the  square the next part to solve it would  be then to take the square root of both  sides of the equation  and when you do that you get two answers  you get plus or minus 4 and this is  going to just be X plus 3 because the  square and the square root cancel so now  what you can do is you can go ahead and  split this into two equations so I would  write this as X plus 3 is equal to  positive 4 and X plus 3 is equal to  negative 4 so if we subtract 3 from both  sides we get x equals 1 and here if we  subtract 3 from both sides we get x  equals negative 7 and those are our two  solutions now that was an easy problem  because we had a leading coefficient of  1 but how would you do a little bit more  difficult problem say for example like  this  five x squared minus 10x minus 15 equals  zero  well BC this leading coefficient is five  in front what you want to do is you want  to divide everything by five okay so  this way it's a little bit easier to  complete the square so now you have x  squared minus two X minus three equals  zero I would move that constant to the  other side by doing the op so that would  add three so now we have x squared minus  two x equals three and then to complete  the square we take a half of that middle  coefficient half of that number in front  of the ax and we square it so sometimes  I like to do that work off to the side  so negative two divided by two and  squared so that's going to be negative  one squared which is equal to one so if  we add one to the left side we want to  keep that balance and add one to the  right side the left side of the equation  when we do this completing the square it  becomes a perfect square okay or a  binomial squared and the way you factor  that remember it's always going to be  half of that middle coefficient so in  this case instead of negative two we're  going to take half of that it's going to  be X minus 1 the quantity squared equals  four if you want to check your work you  can write this twice X minus one times X  minus one and foil it and you'll get  back to x squared minus 2x plus one  but now we've completed the square all  we need to do is take the square root of  both sides of the equation that's going  to give us remember two answers plus or  minus two the square and the square root  cancel and I'm going to show you another  method on this problem instead of  splitting it up into two equations what  I'm gonna do is I'm going to add one to  both sides of the equation and when I do  that what you're gonna see is that this  is going to be plus one plus one so  that's going to be x equals one plus or  minus two so notice I put that adding  one I put that plus one in front of the  plus or minus you don't want to put it  over here on the right and so now we  have two problems to solve one plus two  is of course three and one minus two is  negative one and those are going to be  our two solutions to that  one the third example I want to show you  now involves some fractions so a little  bit more challenging example we've got  negative 2x squared plus 5x minus 1  equals 0 and what we want to do here is  we want to again get that leading  coefficient to be positive 1 so I'm  going to divide everything by a negative  2 it makes it easier to complete the  square of course 0 divided by negative 2  is still going to be 0 this is going to  be 1/2 this is going to be negative 5  halves okay and then this is going to be  just these cancel it just gives us  positive x squared so now what I would  do is I would get the number on the  other side by subtracting 1/2 from both  sides of the equation and that's going  to give us x squared minus 5 halves  equals a negative 1/2 so here's the  completing the square part what we want  to do is we want to take half of  negative 5 over 2 and square it now when  you take half of something you can  divide it by 2 or you can multiply it by  1/2 so in this case this comes out to  negative 5/4 squared which is 25 16 so  we're going to add 25 16 to the right  side of the equation and we're going to  add 25 16 to the left side to keep it  balanced so now we just have to factor  the left side and remember when you  factor it's going to be a perfect square  so it's going to be X and then half of  this middle coefficient so it's gonna be  X minus 5 over 4 I just multiplied this  by 1/2 on the right side though we have  to get a common denominator so it looks  like if we multiply the numerator and  denominator by 8 that's going to give us  negative 8 16 plus 25 16 which is equal  to let's see 1716  now we've completed the square all we  need to do is solve by taking the square  root of both sides of the equation and  remember when you do that you get your  two answers of course square root of 17  we can't simplify but the square root of  16 is just going to be 4 and then here  the square and the square root cancel  one another out we just are left with X  minus 5/4 and the last thing to do now  is really just to get the X by itself so  I'm going to add five force to both  sides of the equation and remember what  you want to do is you want to put that  5/4 in front of the plus or minus so  it's going to be 5/4 plus or minus the  square root of 17 over 4 and you can do  this on your calculator or you can leave  it like this as an exact answer

